<ArticleLayout slug="turnaround-days-not-weeks" ctaLabel="Check a timeline" faq={faq}>
  <p class="standfirst">A production calendar runs two clocks at once: the time the work takes, and the time it waits for a decision. Faster production shortens only the first one.</p>

  <h2>How long does content production take?</h2>

  <p>No method-stated benchmark answers that. The windows in circulation are published by companies selling the work, and each one starts its clock somewhere different. What can be shown is where the days go: making compresses, waiting for decisions does not. hubStudio publishes its own windows, and they are quoted below with their pages.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Across four buyer searches on content production turnaround, 35 results came back. Every one was published by a company selling production, editing, photography, software, tools or training, or by a practitioner, a content farm or an off-topic manufacturer. None came from a trade body, a statistical agency or a peer-reviewed journal, and none of the stage timelines read in full stated a sample or a method.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: search-results audit run 10 September 2026 across four English buyer phrasings, publisher type recorded for every result, three stage-timeline pages read in full, no domain named.</p>

  <p>So this page does not print an industry number. It prints the stages, what each one lets you compress, and the arithmetic that decides the rest.</p>

  <h2>Where do the days go, stage by stage?</h2>

  <p>The table below is production practice: a description of how an engagement runs, not a measurement. Where a figure exists for a stage, it is quoted further down with its source.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Stage</th>
          <th scope="col">What compresses</th>
          <th scope="col">What does not</th>
          <th scope="col">Who owns the wait</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Brief</th>
          <td>The length of the document</td>
          <td>Agreement on what the brief means</td>
          <td>The buyer</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Proposal</th>
          <td>The studio's response time</td>
          <td>The buyer's internal budget sign-off</td>
          <td>The studio, then the buyer</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Scope lock</th>
          <td>Very little, because it is a decision</td>
          <td>Adding a market or format later reopens it</td>
          <td>Both sides</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Capture</th>
          <td>Shoot days on standard set-ups</td>
          <td>Sample shipping, customs and public holidays</td>
          <td>The buyer for samples, the studio for the shoot</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Model training</th>
          <td>Compute time</td>
          <td>Clearing the training set, and the depth of the brand library</td>
          <td>The studio, working on the buyer's library</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Generation</th>
          <td>The generation call itself</td>
          <td>Selecting and finishing what comes back</td>
          <td>The studio</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Review rounds</th>
