  </div>

  <p>Read down the third column and a pattern shows. Almost everything that refuses to compress is a decision, a queue or a calendar someone else controls.</p>

  <p>The first loop usually starts at the brief. The one trade-body release on the subject measures perception, not days, and it is five years old, but it is consistent.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">In a survey of more than 1,700 marketers and agency staff across more than 70 countries, 69 percent of marketers and 73 percent of agency respondents agreed that rebriefs happen too often. The same survey found 78 percent of marketers believed their briefs gave clear strategic direction, against 5 percent of creative agency respondents.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: global briefing survey released by the UK advertising agency trade body, 13 October 2021; self-reported agreement, fielding window and per-group counts not published, and it measures perception, not time.</p>

  <h2>Where does a quoted turnaround start?</h2>

  <p>Before comparing two quoted windows, ask where each clock starts and what it leaves out. Published schedules tend to start late and stop before your review.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">One Chinese ecommerce photography studio's published process, dated July 2026, gives 5 to 7 working days for white-background and scene images and 2 to 4 weeks for video. Its own step list shows where that clock starts: a shoot of one to two days, a day of image selection, two to three days of retouch and a day to deliver. Contract signature and one to three days of sample shipping come before it, and the two free revisions it offers carry no time at all.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: a Chinese ecommerce photography studio's published eight-step process page, dated 20 July 2026, read in Chinese 10 September 2026, one studio's own terms; the clock-start reading is arithmetic on its stated steps.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">A Chinese video production company's published schedule runs about 20 to 24 working days from first client conversation to delivery, including three working days to adjust the cut after feedback, and states that its post-production estimate does not include the client's review and revisions.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: a Chinese video production company's published schedule, dated 7 August 2019, read in Chinese 10 September 2026, one company's terms and seven years old; the day total is arithmetic on its six stated stages.</p>

  <p>The fastest promises attach to the smallest jobs. Design subscriptions are the clearest case.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Turnaround promises in this category are published against small or minor requests, not against whole jobs. Cards read in September 2026 publish next-day, same-day, 24-hour and 12-hour promises, each qualified by request size or tier.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: published pricing pages in this category, September 2026, read directly, no vendor named.</p>

  <p>And the stages that most often stretch a calendar are the ones nobody prices.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">None of eight published price surfaces read across two markets in September 2026 prices sample logistics, reshoots or approval rounds.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: published pricing pages in this category, two markets, read directly September 2026, absence confirmed by reading each card in full, no vendor named.</p>

  <p>Retouch is the exception, because it is the one stage you can buy speed on.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Retouch is the stage a buyer can speed up by paying. One published image editing card read in September 2026 sets a 24-hour default and sells turnaround from 6 to 96 hours; another guarantees 200 edited images a day with next-morning delivery, and quotes a 600-a-day tier from a 3-hour turnaround on custom pricing.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: two published image editing pricing pages in this category, read directly 9 and 10 September 2026, turnaround terms as each card states them, no vendor named.</p>

  <h2>How fast can AI content production turn around?</h2>

  <p>Generation is fast. Finishing is not, and the published record says so.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Published documentation for one image generation model states that complex prompts "may take up to 2 minutes to process." Two published documentation pages for video generation models, read the same day, state no generation time at all.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: published model documentation in this category, read 10 September 2026, pages undated or last updated June 2026, no vendor named; a stated ceiling for complex prompts, not a measured average.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">On one documented AI-assisted short film, a three-person team took about a week and a half to two weeks to finish roughly 90 seconds of footage. Its post-production lead put generated material to finished footage at about 300 to 1, with rotoscoping, artifact removal, retiming, grading and grain still required.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: a visual-effects trade publication's interview with the production team, April 2024; one production, the practitioner's own estimate, no counting log, and a dated model generation.</p>

  <p>Two minutes of generation against two weeks of finishing is one production, not a rule. It does show where the calendar goes once the model is no longer the slow part: selection, cleanup and the decisions around them.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Forty percent of in-house agency leads told a global advertiser trade body they had seen faster content production from AI. The research reports speed as a share of respondents saying so, not as days saved.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: global advertiser trade body research on AI in in-house agencies, 4 November 2025; a quantitative survey plus 30 in-depth interviews, respondent count not published on the page.</p>

  <h2>What do review rounds add to the calendar?</h2>

  <p>More than most schedules admit, and it can be worked out without a benchmark.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">No method-stated benchmark for how long creative approval takes, or for how many review rounds a project runs, turned up in searches run in September 2026. The one numeric approval benchmark located disclosed no sample size.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: evidence searches run 10 September 2026 in English and Chinese, every result classified by publisher type and the one numeric benchmark read in full; a scoped observation, not proof that none exists.</p>

  <p>In place of a benchmark, use the arithmetic. Review time added to one deliverable equals the number of rounds multiplied by the wait for consolidated feedback plus the revision work. The inputs below are round numbers chosen for illustration, not measurements. Put your own in.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Scenario</th>
          <th scope="col">Rounds</th>
          <th scope="col">Wait per round</th>
          <th scope="col">Revision per round</th>
          <th scope="col">Review time added</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Next-day feedback</th>
          <td>3</td>
          <td>1 day</td>
          <td>1 day</td>
          <td>6 working days</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Feedback in three days</th>
          <td>3</td>
          <td>3 days</td>
          <td>1 day</td>
          <td>12 working days</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Weekly review meeting</th>
          <td>3</td>
          <td>5 days</td>
          <td>1 day</td>
          <td>18 working days</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Weekly meeting, one extra round</th>
          <td>4</td>
          <td>5 days</td>
          <td>1 day</td>
          <td>24 working days</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Weekly meeting, revision twice as fast</th>
          <td>3</td>
          <td>5 days</td>
          <td>0.5 day</td>
          <td>16.5 working days</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Review time grows with the number of rounds multiplied by the wait for consolidated feedback plus the revision work. At three rounds and a day of revision each, next-day feedback adds six working days, three-day feedback adds twelve, and a weekly review meeting adds eighteen. A fourth round at the weekly pace takes it to twenty-four, while halving revision time in that case saves a day and a half.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: arithmetic with illustrative inputs, not measurements, 10 September 2026, shown so a reader can substitute their own.</p>

  <p>The last row is the one to show whoever wants a faster studio. Halving the studio's revision time saves a day and a half. Moving from a weekly meeting to next-day feedback saves twelve.</p>

  <p>Time zones decide how long that wait is before anyone has an opinion.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">China runs on a single time zone, UTC+8. A nine-to-six working day in Shanghai overlaps a nine-to-six day in London by about one hour in winter and two in summer, in Paris or Berlin by two and three, and in Sydney by about seven hours on standard time and six on daylight time. It overlaps Los Angeles by one hour in the northern winter and not at all in summer. It never overlaps a New York working day.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: published civil time offsets, arithmetic shown so the reader can check it, September 2026.</p>

  <p>With no overlap, comments sent at the end of a New York day land at the start of the Shanghai day, which works well for written feedback. A round that needs a live conversation has to wait for a call booked outside one side's working hours.</p>

  <h2>What closes the calendar that nobody on the project controls?</h2>

  <p>Public holidays, and platform deadlines that publish no lead time.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">China's 2026 holiday schedule stopped work for nine days over the Spring Festival, 15 to 23 February, for five days at Labor Day, 1 to 5 May, and for seven days at National Day, 1 to 7 October. Weekend days were worked in exchange: 14 and 28 February, 9 May, 20 September and 10 October.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the State Council General Office notice on 2026 holiday arrangements, issued 4 November 2025.</p>

  <p>National Day falls on 1 to 7 October 2026, so a project briefed into China in late September loses that week before its first review.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">No platform rule, government notice or survey found in September 2026 publishes how long creative production should take before a Singles Day or 618 deadline. The lead times in circulation come from companies selling preparation services, with no sample and no method behind them.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: search of Chinese- and English-language sources, 10 September 2026, covering reporting of platform rules and the pages ranking for four buyer phrasings. An observation about the published record, not a measurement.</p>

  <h2>What does hubStudio publish about its own turnaround?</h2>

  <p>Policy lines and ranges, each on the page that publishes it, none of them a measured average.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">hubStudio publishes that it sends a binding proposal within 48 hours of receiving a brief. That is published studio policy, not a measured average.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: hubStudio pricing page, read 10 September 2026, published studio policy.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">hubStudio publishes three rounds of modification on finalized work for asset and project engagements, and more on retainer.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: hubStudio pricing page, read 10 September 2026, published studio policy.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">hubStudio's video production page publishes 24 to 72 hours from brief to first cut on simple social cuts and AIGC spots, one to two weeks from brief to delivery on standard studio shoots including pre-production, shoot day and post, and two to four weeks on location productions including travel, scouting and post.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: hubStudio video production service page, read 10 September 2026, published ranges with no sample of engagements behind them.</p>

  <p>Where a brand model is needed, its training sits before generation, not beside it.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">hubStudio publishes a window of three to six weeks from brief to a trained custom model, depending on the depth of the brand library. It is a published range, not a measured benchmark: no sample of engagements sits behind it.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: hubStudio AI excellence page, September 2026.</p>

  <p>Individual engagements show the range. They are single projects as the studio reports them, not typical durations.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">hubStudio's case study for 1834 Gin, a new Philippine premium spirit, reports going from no brand imagery and no footage to an omnichannel launch in three weeks, built around a 46-second brand film made with AIGC and CGI. One engagement as the studio reports it, not a typical duration.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: hubStudio 1834 Gin case study page, read 10 September 2026, published case result.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">hubStudio's case study for Mexicash, a Mexican micro-credit lender, reports a video campaign across five platforms taken from concept to finished campaign in five days, with cuts at six, eight, twelve and fifteen seconds. One engagement as the studio reports it, not a typical duration.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: hubStudio Mexicash case study page, read 10 September 2026, published case result.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">hubStudio's case study for a premium automotive manufacturer reports launch imagery for fifteen markets, across more than twenty generated environments, produced in three weeks with no vehicle shipped. One engagement as the studio reports it, not a typical duration.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: hubStudio premium SUV case study page, read 10 September 2026, published case result.</p>

  <h2>A turnaround checklist to use on the next brief</h2>

  <p>Production practice, not a standard. None of these lines carries a figure, and each one removes waiting rather than work.</p>

  <p>- Name one approver per deliverable, and one person who consolidates comments. - Write the number of rounds into the scope, and define a round as one consolidated set of comments, not a thread. - Book review slots in both calendars before kickoff, and put the feedback turnaround in working days into the scope. - Check the working-hour overlap between your reviewers and the studio, then choose written or call-based review. - Confirm the sample ship date, and whether the samples cross a border. - Check public holidays in the production country across the whole window. - Decide at the brief whether a trained model is needed. If it is, schedule it before generation. - Ask where each quoted window starts: brief, signature, sample receipt or shoot. - Ask whether a quoted schedule includes your own review time. - Ask the price and the daily volume cap of any faster retouch tier. - Lock formats and markets before the first review. - Put a date on final approval, not only on final delivery.</p>

  <p>The <a href="/pricing">pricing page</a> sets out how an engagement is scoped, the <a href="/the-studio/ai-excellence">AI excellence page</a> covers the training window, the <a href="/work">work index</a> holds the full case studies, and the <a href="/resources/insights/subscription-or-managed-production">subscription or managed production article</a> explains what turnaround promises are attached to.</p>

  <p>Reviewed September 2026.</p>

  <h2>Questions about production turnaround</h2>

  <h3>How long does content production take?</h3>

  <p>No method-stated benchmark exists in the published record, and quoted windows start their clocks in different places. As one published example, hubStudio's video production page gives 24 to 72 hours from brief to first cut on simple social cuts and one to two weeks on standard studio shoots. Always ask whether a quote includes your own review time.</p>

  <h3>What slows down a creative production project?</h3>

  <p>Waiting, more than making. Each review round adds the wait for consolidated feedback on top of the revision work, so three rounds at a weekly meeting add eighteen working days in illustrative arithmetic. The brief is the other usual cause: in a 2021 trade body survey, 69 percent of marketers agreed that rebriefs happen too often.</p>

  <h3>How many revision rounds are normal?</h3>

  <p>No method-stated norm turned up in searches run in September 2026. hubStudio publishes three rounds of modification on finalized work, with more on retainer. Whatever the number, define a round in the scope as one consolidated set of comments, because a round that arrives as a thread of separate notes behaves like several.</p>

  <h3>How fast can AI content production turn around?</h3>

  <p>The generation step is quick: one image model's documentation puts complex prompts at up to two minutes. Finishing is slower. One documented AI-assisted short took a three-person team a week and a half to two weeks for about 90 seconds of film, with cleanup, retiming and grading still required.</p>

  <h3>How do I shorten approval time?</h3>

  <p>Name one approver and one person who consolidates comments, book review slots before kickoff, write the number of rounds into the scope, and put final approval on the calendar. On the illustrative arithmetic, moving three rounds from a weekly meeting to next-day feedback saves twelve working days, far more than faster revisions would.</p>
